long_physics_sentences = [
    "The speed of light in a vacuum is constant. It is approximately 299,792,458 meters per second and represents a fundamental limit for the transmission of information and energy in the universe. This constancy underpins Einstein's theory of relativity, linking space and time and influencing the behavior of objects moving at high velocities. The invariance of light speed also allows precise measurements in physics experiments and technologies such as GPS and astronomy.",
    
    "Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed. This principle, known as the conservation of energy, implies that the total energy of an isolated system remains constant, although it can change forms between kinetic, potential, thermal, and other types. It is a cornerstone of classical and modern physics, appearing in mechanical systems, chemical reactions, and even cosmological processes. Understanding energy transformations allows engineers and scientists to design efficient machines and predict the behavior of natural phenomena accurately.",
    
    "Force equals mass times acceleration. This fundamental relationship, expressed by Newton's second law, quantifies how the motion of an object changes in response to applied forces. It implies that heavier objects require more force to accelerate at the same rate as lighter objects and that the same force will produce different accelerations depending on mass. This law is foundational in mechanics, enabling the prediction of trajectories, collision outcomes, and the dynamics of physical systems.",
    
    "Every action has an equal and opposite reaction. This statement, Newton's third law, explains that forces always occur in pairs, where one object's push or pull results in a corresponding force on the other object. It is observable in phenomena ranging from the recoil of a gun to the propulsion of rockets, as the expulsion of mass produces thrust in the opposite direction. Recognizing these action-reaction pairs is essential for understanding equilibrium, motion, and mechanical systems in engineering and physics.",
    
    "The laws of thermodynamics govern energy transfer. They describe how energy moves and transforms within physical systems, imposing limits on processes such as heat flow and work extraction. The first law ensures energy conservation, while the second law introduces the concept of entropy, dictating the direction of spontaneous processes. These principles are critical in fields like chemistry, engineering, and environmental science, influencing engine efficiency, refrigeration, and even the evolution of the universe.",
    
    "Electric charges exert forces on each other. According to Coulomb's law, like charges repel while opposite charges attract, with the force magnitude depending on the product of the charges and the inverse square of the distance between them. This interaction is responsible for electric fields, currents, and the behavior of atoms and molecules. Understanding these forces is fundamental for designing electronic circuits, understanding chemical bonding, and explaining electromagnetic phenomena.",
    
    "The momentum of a system is conserved in isolation. This means that in the absence of external forces, the total momentum—calculated as mass times velocity—remains constant, regardless of internal interactions. Conservation of momentum explains why objects recoil when forces are exchanged, as in collisions, and is critical in analyzing particle interactions in physics. It provides a framework for solving problems in mechanics, astrophysics, and even quantum systems.",
    
    "Waves can interfere constructively or destructively. When two waves meet, their amplitudes combine, resulting in reinforcement or cancellation depending on their relative phase. This principle is central to understanding phenomena such as sound patterns, light diffraction, and quantum wave behavior. Interference is also applied practically in technologies like noise-canceling headphones, optical instruments, and radio signal processing.",
    
    "Gravitational attraction exists between all masses. Every object with mass exerts a pull on every other mass, proportional to their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance separating them, as described by Newton's law of universal gravitation. This force governs planetary orbits, tidal effects, and the structure of galaxies, providing a predictable framework for celestial mechanics. Understanding gravity is essential for spacecraft navigation, satellite deployment, and studying the dynamics of the cosmos.",
    
    "Quantum particles exhibit both wave and particle behavior. This duality means that particles like electrons and photons can display interference and diffraction, characteristic of waves, while also showing localized impacts like particles. It is a fundamental feature of quantum mechanics, challenging classical intuition and requiring probabilistic descriptions of physical systems. This behavior underlies technologies such as semiconductors, lasers, and quantum computing, reshaping our understanding of matter and energy at microscopic scales.",

    "Electric currents generate magnetic fields. This relationship, described by Ampère’s law and unified in Maxwell’s equations, explains how moving charges produce magnetic effects in surrounding space. The interaction between electricity and magnetism forms the basis of electromagnetism, governing motors, generators, and electromagnetic waves. This principle is fundamental to modern power systems, communication technologies, and electronic devices.",

    "Entropy tends to increase in closed systems. This concept reflects the natural progression toward disorder and energy dispersion, defining the arrow of time in physical processes. As entropy increases, systems become less capable of performing useful work without external input. This principle explains why perpetual motion machines are impossible and why heat flows spontaneously from hot objects to cold ones.",

    "Pressure in fluids is transmitted uniformly in all directions. Known as Pascal’s principle, this behavior allows forces applied to confined fluids to be amplified and redirected. It underpins the operation of hydraulic systems such as lifts, brakes, and presses. Understanding fluid pressure transmission is essential in mechanical engineering and fluid dynamics.",

    "Objects in free fall experience constant acceleration near Earth’s surface. This acceleration, approximately 9.81 meters per second squared, is independent of an object’s mass when air resistance is negligible. It explains the predictable motion of falling bodies and projectile trajectories. This principle is central to classical mechanics and experimental physics demonstrations.",

    "Electromagnetic waves propagate through a vacuum. Unlike mechanical waves, they do not require a medium and travel at the speed of light. This property enables the transmission of light, radio waves, X-rays, and other forms of radiation across vast distances in space. It is foundational to astronomy, wireless communication, and modern imaging technologies.",

    "The Pauli exclusion principle restricts identical fermions. It states that no two fermions can occupy the same quantum state simultaneously, shaping the structure of atoms and matter. This principle explains electron configurations, chemical properties, and the stability of matter. It is essential for understanding solid-state physics and atomic behavior.",

    "Acceleration can occur without changes in speed. When an object changes direction while maintaining constant speed, such as in circular motion, it still experiences acceleration. This acceleration is directed toward the center of the circular path and is known as centripetal acceleration. It is crucial for analyzing orbits, rotating systems, and mechanical stability.",

    "Light exhibits refraction when passing between media. Changes in propagation speed cause light rays to bend at boundaries, as described by Snell’s law. Refraction explains phenomena such as lenses focusing light and the apparent bending of objects in water. This behavior is fundamental to optics, vision correction, and imaging systems.",

    "Mass and energy are equivalent. Expressed by Einstein’s equation E = mc², this relationship shows that mass can be converted into energy and vice versa. It explains the enormous energy released in nuclear reactions and stellar processes. This equivalence reshaped modern physics and underpins nuclear power and particle physics.",

    "Quantum states evolve probabilistically. The outcomes of measurements in quantum mechanics cannot be predicted with certainty, only with statistical probabilities. This probabilistic nature contrasts with classical determinism and requires wavefunctions to describe physical systems. It is essential for understanding atomic transitions, tunneling, and quantum measurement."
]

long_btc_analysis = [
    "Analyse BTC/USD and identify breakouts. This involves monitoring price movements to detect when Bitcoin breaks through significant resistance or support levels, signaling potential strong directional moves. Breakouts often indicate increased market momentum and can precede substantial price trends, making them crucial for both short-term trading and long-term positioning. Traders use technical indicators, volume analysis, and chart patterns to confirm the validity of these breakouts before taking positions.",
    
    "Analyse BTC/USD and detect trend reversals. Identifying trend reversals requires examining price action, candlestick patterns, and momentum indicators to anticipate changes in the prevailing market direction. Recognizing reversals early allows traders to adjust positions, manage risk, and capture potential profit opportunities from emerging bullish or bearish trends. Combining technical tools like RSI, MACD, and moving averages enhances the accuracy of reversal detection in volatile cryptocurrency markets.",
    
    "Analyse BTC/USD and spot support levels. Support levels are price points where buying interest tends to prevent further declines, forming a foundation for price stability. Identifying these levels helps traders set entry points, stop-loss orders, and risk management strategies. Historical price data, trendlines, and volume analysis are commonly used to validate these support zones, providing insights into potential market reversals or bounces.",
    
    "Analyse BTC/USD and spot resistance levels. Resistance levels are price areas where selling pressure often halts upward movement, acting as barriers to further gains. Detecting these levels allows traders to anticipate potential reversals or breakout attempts and plan exits or short positions accordingly. Technical tools such as Fibonacci retracements, previous highs, and trendline analysis help identify and confirm significant resistance zones.",
    
    "Analyse BTC/USD and track moving averages. Moving averages smooth out price data over specified periods to highlight underlying trends and market direction. Monitoring short-term and long-term moving averages helps identify bullish or bearish momentum, potential crossovers, and dynamic support or resistance. Traders often combine moving averages with other indicators to make informed entry, exit, and risk management decisions in the Bitcoin market.",
    
    "Analyse BTC/USD and identify volume spikes. Volume spikes indicate sudden increases in trading activity, often signaling strong buying or selling interest. These spikes can confirm the strength of breakouts, reversals, or emerging trends, providing insights into market sentiment. Analyzing volume alongside price movements helps traders assess the sustainability of trends and potential volatility in the BTC/USD pair.",
    
    "Analyse BTC/USD and detect price consolidations. Price consolidation occurs when Bitcoin trades within a narrow range, reflecting market indecision and reduced volatility. Recognizing these periods is important because they often precede significant breakout moves, allowing traders to anticipate potential trend continuation or reversal. Technical analysis tools like Bollinger Bands, support/resistance zones, and volume patterns help identify and confirm consolidations.",
    
    "Analyse BTC/USD and highlight key price zones. Key price zones include areas of historical support, resistance, and liquidity concentration where significant market activity occurs. Highlighting these zones allows traders to anticipate potential reaction points, manage risk, and plan strategic trades. Combining historical price data, volume analysis, and technical indicators improves the accuracy of identifying these critical market levels.",
    
    "Analyse BTC/USD and monitor volatility shifts. Volatility shifts reflect changes in the intensity and frequency of price fluctuations, influencing risk and trading strategies. By tracking indicators like ATR, Bollinger Bands, and standard deviation, traders can detect periods of heightened or reduced volatility. Understanding these shifts allows for better timing of trades, position sizing, and anticipation of potential market breakouts or consolidations.",
    
    "Analyse BTC/USD and identify breakout patterns. Breakout patterns involve chart formations such as triangles, flags, and rectangles that indicate potential imminent price surges or drops. Detecting these patterns provides traders with actionable signals for entering or exiting trades ahead of significant market moves. Combining pattern recognition with volume analysis and trend confirmation enhances the reliability of breakout predictions in the BTC/USD market.",

    "Analyse BTC/USD and assess market structure shifts. Market structure analysis focuses on identifying changes in higher highs, higher lows, lower highs, and lower lows to determine trend integrity. Structural breaks often signal trend continuation or invalidation, providing early directional clues. Traders rely on swing analysis and key level breaches to validate structural changes in Bitcoin price action.",

    "Analyse BTC/USD and evaluate momentum divergence. Momentum divergence occurs when price action and indicators such as RSI or MACD move in opposite directions. This condition can signal weakening trends or potential reversals before price reacts. Identifying divergence helps traders anticipate exhaustion points and adjust exposure accordingly.",

    "Analyse BTC/USD and monitor funding rate behavior. Funding rates in perpetual futures markets reflect trader positioning and sentiment bias. Extreme positive or negative funding can indicate overcrowded trades and increased reversal risk. Incorporating funding analysis alongside technical structure improves context for BTC/USD trade decisions.",

    "Analyse BTC/USD and track on-chain supply dynamics. On-chain metrics such as exchange inflows, outflows, and long-term holder behavior provide insight into underlying supply pressure. Shifts in these metrics can precede significant price movements by indicating accumulation or distribution phases. Combining on-chain data with technical analysis enhances market conviction.",

    "Analyse BTC/USD and identify liquidity sweeps. Liquidity sweeps occur when price briefly moves beyond key highs or lows to trigger stop orders before reversing. These events often signal institutional participation and short-term market manipulation. Recognizing liquidity grabs helps traders avoid false breakouts and refine entry timing.",

    "Analyse BTC/USD and assess trend exhaustion zones. Trend exhaustion zones develop after extended directional moves accompanied by declining momentum and volume. These zones often precede corrective phases or full reversals. Traders use oscillators, volume decay, and price extensions to identify exhaustion conditions.",

    "Analyse BTC/USD and monitor correlation with macro assets. Bitcoin frequently exhibits correlation shifts with equities, bonds, and the US dollar during macro-driven markets. Tracking these relationships provides context for risk-on or risk-off behavior. Understanding correlation regimes helps traders anticipate broader capital flow impacts on BTC/USD.",

    "Analyse BTC/USD and evaluate volatility compression. Volatility compression is characterized by narrowing price ranges and contracting indicators such as Bollinger Bands. These conditions often precede explosive directional moves. Detecting compression phases allows traders to prepare for breakout scenarios with defined risk.",

    "Analyse BTC/USD and identify trend channel boundaries. Trend channels define the upper and lower bounds of sustained price movement. Trading near channel extremes provides favorable risk-to-reward opportunities for continuation or mean reversion setups. Channel analysis helps structure trades within dominant market trends.",

    "Analyse BTC/USD and assess sentiment extremes. Sentiment indicators derived from derivatives positioning, fear and greed metrics, and social data can highlight emotional market conditions. Extreme optimism or pessimism often coincides with local tops or bottoms. Integrating sentiment analysis with technical signals improves timing accuracy."
]



